; ACE Shell-Startup
;
; The per-shell script. On a real Amiga S:Startup-Sequence is the boot script,
; run once by the Initial CLI, and this one runs for every shell started after
; it. ACE has one kind of shell and no boot to tell them apart, so it runs
; both, in that order, then S:ACE-Startup -- see run_startup_scripts() in
; src/ace_user_shell.c. Aliases belong here rather than in the boot script
; because an alias lives in the shell that defined it and in no other.
;
; Anything in here is overwritten by the next install. S:User-Startup is not,
; which is the whole point of it, and is where a change of your own belongs.

; Clear the screen, the way an Amiga spells it.
;
; "*E" is the AmigaDOS star-escape for ESC, so this is ESC [ 0 ; 0 H followed
; by ESC [ J: home the cursor, then erase from there to the end of the
; display. Both halves are needed. Erasing without homing leaves the cursor
; where it was and clears only what follows it, which on a full screen clears
; nothing at all.
;
; NOLINE because Echo otherwise ends with a newline, which would step the
; cursor off the home position it was just moved to and leave the first line
; blank.
;
; ACE's console accepts four spellings of this -- form feed, native CSI H/CSI
; J, the ESC [ form used here, and ESC c -- all of them checked by
; tests/console_device_bridge_test.c. It deliberately does not accept CSI 2J,
; which is later ANSI rather than the AmigaOS 3.1 command, so do not "simplify"
; this line to Echo "*E[2J".
;
; CLS is the Amiga name and CLEAR is the one Linux fingers type. Two aliases
; rather than one aliased to the other, so that neither depends on the other
; still existing.
Alias CLS Echo "*E[0;0H*E[J" NOLINE
Alias CLEAR Echo "*E[0;0H*E[J" NOLINE
Alias Lx Linux
